course: scandinavian
name: Scandinavian Defence
color: black
eco: B01
tagline: Hit e4 on move one and play your own game from the very start.
description: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5. You trade off White's e-pawn at once and develop around the queen: ...Qa5, ...Nf6, ...c6, ...Bf5 and ...e6. The structure is like a Caro-Kann, but White has to face it on your terms. This course covers 3.Nc3 with all of White's main setups, 3.Nf3, 3.d4 and the early deviations 2.Nc3 and 2.e5, plus the traps to avoid.

chapter: main
title: 3.Nc3 Qa5: the main line
intro: After 3.Nc3 the queen goes to a5, where it pins the c3 knight and stays safe from attacks. Then you develop with ...Nf6, ...c6 and ...Bf5, with ...e6 later. Your c6 pawn gives the queen a way back to c7 and b6.

line: main-bc4
name: Main line: 6.Bc4 Bf5 7.Bd2
moves: 1.e4 d5 {Challenge e4 at once.} 2.exd5 Qxd5 {Recapture with the queen. White gains a tempo with Nc3, but your queen has a safe home.} 3.Nc3 Qa5 {The classical square: the queen pins the c3 knight and stays out of reach.} 4.d4 Nf6 5.Nf3 c6 {Gives the queen a retreat to c7 or d8, and keeps an eye on b5 and d5.} 6.Bc4 Bf5 {Develop the bishop outside the pawn chain, as in the Caro-Kann.} 7.Bd2 e6 {Solid. Your structure is very hard to crack.} 8.Qe2 Bb4 {Pin the knight again and prepare to castle.} 9.a3 Nbd7 {Calm: keep the bishop until White commits.} 10.O-O-O Bxc3 11.Bxc3 Qc7 {The queen is safe and Black is solid and comfortable.}

line: main-ne5
name: 7.Ne5: White goes for the kingside
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3 Qa5 4.d4 Nf6 5.Nf3 c6 6.Bc4 Bf5 7.Ne5 {Ideas like g4 and Bd2 with tricks against your queen.} e6 8.g4 Bg6 9.h4 Nbd7 {Challenge the e5 knight.} 10.Nxd7 Nxd7 11.h5 Be4 {The bishop is active on e4, and Black is fine.}

line: main-bd2
name: 5.Bd2 before Nf3
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3 Qa5 4.d4 Nf6 5.Bd2 {White unpins and prepares tricks with Nd5.} c6 6.Bc4 Qc7 {Step away from the b5 and d5 tricks.} 7.Qe2 Bf5 8.Nf3 e6 9.O-O-O Nbd7 {Solid as always.}

line: main-nf3-first
name: 4.Nf3 move order
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3 Qa5 4.Nf3 Nf6 5.d4 c6 {Same position as the main line.} 6.Bc4 Bf5

line: bg4-trap
name: 4.Bc4: avoid the ...Bg4 trap
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3 Qa5 4.Bc4 Nf6 5.Nf3 c6 {Before ...Bf5 or ...Bg4, play ...c6. After 5...Bg4? 6.h3 Bh5 7.g4 Bg6 8.Ne5, the f7 square collapses.} 6.O-O Bf5 7.d3 e6 8.Bd2 Qc7 {The standard setup again.}

chapter: alternatives
title: White's third-move alternatives
intro: White doesn't have to play 3.Nc3. Against 3.Nf3 your bishop pins with ...Bg4. Against 3.d4 you can punish the queen's position at once with ...e5.

line: nf3
name: 3.Nf3
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White doesn't hit the queen yet.} Nf6 4.d4 Bg4 {Pin the knight.} 5.Be2 e6 6.O-O Nc6 {Pressure on d4.} 7.c4 Qd7 {The queen steps back, watching d4. Plan: ...O-O-O.}

line: nf3-be2
name: 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Be2
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Be2 {Slow development.} Bg4 {Same idea: pin and develop.} 5.O-O Nc6 6.d3 O-O-O {Long castling with active pieces.}

line: d4
name: 3.d4: hit the centre with ...e5
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.d4 e5 {Punish White for not developing: open the centre while your queen is active.} 4.dxe5 Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1 Nc6 {White can't castle and e5 will fall.} 6.Nf3 Bg4 7.Be2 O-O-O+ {Development with check.} 8.Ke1 Nge7 {Win e5 back with ...Ng6. Black is very active.}

chapter: early
title: Early deviations: 2.Nc3 and 2.e5
intro: Some White players don't take on d5. Against 2.e5 you play a Caro-Kann-style Advance with ...Bf5. Against 2.Nc3 you can push ...d4 and grab space.

line: two-e5
name: 2.e5: the Advance
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.e5 {White closes the centre.} Bf5 {Develop the bishop first, just like the Caro-Kann.} 3.d4 e6 4.Nf3 c5 {Hit the chain.} 5.c3 Nc6 6.Be2 Qb6 {Pressure on d4 and b2.}

line: two-nc3
name: 2.Nc3: space with ...d4
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.Nc3 {Defend e4 instead of trading.} d4 {Kick the knight and take space.} 3.Nce2 e5 4.Ng3 Be6 {Develop. The d4 pawn cramps White.} 5.Nf3 f6 {Hold e5. A solid space advantage.}

line: two-d3
name: 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3 Qa5 4.d3?!
moves: 1.e4 d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 3.Nc3 Qa5 4.d3 Nf6 5.Nf3 Bg4 {With d3 played there's no Bc4 trick: pin freely.} 6.Be2 Nc6 7.O-O e6 {Finish development first. Castling long right now walks into b4-b5 and Nd2 tricks against your queen.} 8.h3 Bh5 {Keep the pin. Next ...Be7 and ...O-O or ...O-O-O once the queen has a safe square.}

quiz: main-bc4 6
q: White kicked your queen with 3.Nc3. Why put it on a5?
a: It pins the c3 knight against the king and is hard to attack there
w: It attacks the a2 pawn
w: It prepares ...Qxa2 and ...Qxb1
why: On a5 the queen pins the knight, can't easily be hit by minor pieces, and can drop back to c7 or d8 after ...c6.

quiz: bg4-trap 10
q: Why play ...c6 before developing the bishop to g4?
a: After ...Bg4? h3 Bh5 g4 Bg6 Ne5, White's knight and bishop gang up on f7
w: To prepare ...b5 and attack the bishop
w: It's only a waiting move
why: With White's bishop on c4, ...Bg4 can backfire badly. ...c6 first gives your queen a retreat and you can choose ...Bf5 calmly.

quiz: d4 6
q: White played 3.d4 without developing. What's the strongest answer?
a: 3...e5: open the centre while your queen is active
w: 3...Nf6 and play slowly
w: 3...Qa5+ and grab a pawn
why: 3...e5 4.dxe5 Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1 leaves White's king stuck in the middle, and Black wins the e5 pawn back with excellent development.
